    "system_prompt": false,
    "raw_content": "Now I have a clear picture of the bug:\n\n**Root cause**: In `warmup.py`, the `warmup()` function reads `config.FIRST_BYTE_S` (line 12) but never uses it as a timeout. The `chat()` call on line 13 passes no `timeout_s`, so it defaults to `None`. This means the warmup call inherits no timeout and can hang for up to 600 seconds (the `FIRST_BYTE_S` budget) instead of failing fast.\n\n**The fix**: Pass `timeout_s=config.DEFAULT_WARMUP_TIMEOUT_S` to the `chat()` call in `warmup.py`. This ensures the warmup fails fast (within ~30 seconds by default) if the backend is wedged, rather than waiting for the full suite first-byte budget.\n\n<arch_final>\n{\"status\": \"patched\", \"patch\": \"--- a/warmup.py\\n+++ b/warmup.py\\n@@ -9,5 +9,5 @@ from miniharness import config\\n \\n \\n def warmup(model: str) -> dict[str, Any]:\\n-    _ = config.FIRST_BYTE_S\\n-    return chat(model, \\\"Reply with exactly: OK\\\")\\n+    _ = config.FIRST_BYTE_S\\n+    return chat(model, \\\"Reply with exactly: OK\\\", timeout_s=config.DEFAULT_WARMUP_TIMEOUT_S)\", \"reason\": \"The warmup call did not pass a timeout_s parameter, causing it to inherit no timeout and potentially hang for up to FIRST_BYTE_S (600s) instead of failing fast. By passing timeout_s=config.DEFAULT_WARMUP_TIMEOUT_S (30s default), the warmup will fail quickly if the backend is wedged, matching operator expectations.\", \"citations\": [\"warmup.py:warmup\", \"config.py:DEFAULT_WARMUP_TIMEOUT_S\", \"config.py:FIRST_BYTE_S\"]}\n</arch_final>"
